11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L19",null,{"guide":{"date":"June 27–July 3, 1864","place":"Kennesaw Mountain, Georgia","result":"The June 27 assaults failed, but Sherman’s later flanking movement forced Johnston to leave the Kennesaw lines","facts":[{"label":"Battle result","value":"Confederate tactical victory"},{"label":"U.S. casualties","value":"About 3,000"},{"label":"Confederate casualties","value":"Just over 1,000"},{"label":"Confederate withdrawal detected","value":"Early morning, July 3"},{"label":"Main criticism","value":"The same position was abandoned after a flanking movement less than a week later"}],"maps":[{"title":"Kennesaw Mountain battlefield","caption":"This 1864 drawing places Pigeon Hill on the left, Little Kennesaw in the center, Big Kennesaw on the right, Confederate artillery on the heights, and U.S. forces to the north.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660525/","image":"/images/kennesaw-battlefield-drawing.jpg","alt":"1864 battlefield drawing showing Confederate artillery positions across Kennesaw Mountain"},{"title":"Troop movements on June 27","caption":"The National Park Service map shows the U.S. assault routes, Confederate defensive line, and the relative positions of McPherson, Thomas, Schofield, Loring, Hardee, and Hood.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1539/the-southeast-chronicles-kennesaw-mountain-national-battlefield-park.htm","image":"/images/kennesaw-troop-movements.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of troop movements at Kennesaw Mountain on June 27, 1864"},{"title":"Kennesaw Mountain and the movement toward Smyrna","caption":"The campaign map records the opposing works around Kennesaw and the U.S. movement south around the Confederate left that forced Johnston to withdraw.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008628278/","image":"/images/atlanta-campaign-map-4.jpg","alt":"U.S. Army campaign map showing the lines at Kennesaw Mountain and movement toward Smyrna"},{"title":"Kennesaw Mountain and Marietta today","caption":"The central assault struck south of the mountain near the area now preserved around Cheatham Hill. Other attacks took place at Pigeon Hill and north of the mountain.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-84.67%2C33.86%2C-84.49%2C34.05&layer=mapnik&marker=33.9833%2C-84.5783"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:16","audioTime":76,"title":"The episode returns to Kennesaw Mountain","detail":"Johnston ordered a withdrawal after Sherman moved south around the Confederate left. The retreat began on July 2 and U.S. troops found the works empty early on July 3."},{"time":"2:02","audioTime":122,"title":"U.S. troops occupied the Confederate positions","detail":"George H. Thomas reported the retreat before 3 a.m. John A. Logan’s XV Corps confirmed that Little Kennesaw was vacant."},{"time":"2:39","audioTime":159,"title":"Soldiers saw the graves left by the failed assaults","detail":"Lyman Whitney of the 34th Illinois questioned why a flanking movement had not been used before the June 27 attacks."},{"time":"3:57","audioTime":237,"title":"The episode evaluates the battle","detail":"Kennesaw was a Confederate tactical victory within a campaign that U.S. forces ultimately won. The Confederate position was abandoned less than a week after the assaults."},{"time":"5:03","audioTime":303,"title":"Confederate accounts emphasized the victory","detail":"Officers described high morale, continued confidence in Johnston, and the heavy losses inflicted on the attacking U.S. troops."},{"time":"6:05","audioTime":365,"title":"U.S. soldiers described the attacks as unnecessary","detail":"Veterans criticized the order to attack fortified high ground and continued to question the decision decades later."},{"time":"8:16","audioTime":496,"title":"The episode asks why Sherman attacked","detail":"Sherman usually preferred maneuver, but he believed circumstances sometimes required assaults against strong positions."},{"time":"9:41","audioTime":581,"title":"Chickasaw Bayou provides an earlier comparison","detail":"In December 1862, Sherman attacked fortified heights north of Vicksburg while separated from Grant and lost almost 1,800 men."},{"time":"11:21","audioTime":681,"title":"Tunnel Hill provides a second comparison","detail":"At Chattanooga in November 1863, Sherman attacked Patrick Cleburne’s division on difficult ground and lost just over 1,700 men."},{"time":"14:35","audioTime":875,"title":"The Kennesaw phase had stalled the campaign","detail":"Rain, woods, and expanding Confederate works slowed U.S. flanking movements and kept Sherman’s armies in place."},{"time":"15:40","audioTime":940,"title":"Sherman wanted to prevent reinforcements from leaving Georgia","detail":"Grant had directed Sherman to keep Johnston occupied so Confederate troops could not be sent to reinforce Robert E. Lee in Virginia."},{"time":"17:43","audioTime":1063,"title":"Concern about detached Confederate troops influenced the decision","detail":"Historian Earl J. Hess argues that Sherman feared Johnston could hold Kennesaw with fewer men and send the rest east."},{"time":"19:44","audioTime":1184,"title":"The episode separates strategy from tactics","detail":"Sherman understood large-scale strategic movement, but the planning and coordination of the June 27 attacks were poor."},{"time":"20:09","audioTime":1209,"title":"The later turning movement succeeded","detail":"The movement begun July 1 forced Johnston out without another direct attack, but it temporarily separated much of Sherman’s force from the railroad supply line."},{"time":"24:00","audioTime":1440,"title":"The historical judgment is that the attacks were a mistake","detail":"The episode concludes that Sherman recovered from the failure by resuming maneuver and continuing toward Atlanta."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"William T. Sherman","command":"Military Division of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Ordered the June 27 assaults and the later flanking movement"},{"commander":"George H. Thomas","command":"Army of the Cumberland","subordinates":"Reported the Confederate withdrawal"},{"commander":"John A. Logan","command":"XV Corps","subordinates":"Confirmed the works on Little Kennesaw were empty"},{"commander":"John M. Schofield","command":"Army of the Ohio","subordinates":"Supported a flanking alternative to another frontal assault"},{"commander":"James B. McPherson","command":"Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"Participated in the movement around the Confederate left"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"Joseph E. Johnston","command":"Army of Tennessee","subordinates":"Held Kennesaw and withdrew toward Smyrna"},{"commander":"William J. Hardee","command":"Hardee’s Corps","subordinates":"Defended the Confederate center and left"},{"commander":"John B. Hood","command":"Hood’s Corps","subordinates":"Held the Confederate right"},{"commander":"Patrick R. Cleburne","command":"Cleburne’s Division","subordinates":"Cited in comparison with Sherman’s earlier defeat at Tunnel Hill"}]}],"people":[{"name":"William T. Sherman","role":"Ordered the June 27 assaults and resumed flanking operations after they failed","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph E. Johnston","role":"Won the tactical battle but abandoned the position after his left was turned","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/joseph-e-johnston.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666539/"},{"name":"Lyman Whitney","role":"34th Illinois sergeant who described the graves and questioned the attack","side":"United States"},{"name":"Emerson Opdycke","role":"U.S. colonel who called the planning and execution of the attacks serious blunders","side":"United States"},{"name":"Henry V. Boynton","role":"35th Ohio officer and later critic of the June 27 assault","side":"United States"}],"losses":[{"name":"Charles G. Harker","side":"United States","role":"Brigade commander, IV Corps","outcome":"Killed during the June 27 assault at Cheatham Hill"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/kennesaw-battlefield-drawing.jpg","alt":"Theodore R. Davis drawing of the Confederate position at Kennesaw Mountain","caption":"Theodore R. Davis drew the Confederate artillery positions and the U.S. lines north of Kennesaw during the campaign.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660525/"},{"image":"/images/atlanta-fortifications.jpg","alt":"Confederate earthwork fortifications photographed near Atlanta","caption":"Confederate fieldworks near Atlanta illustrate the kind of earth-and-timber defenses that made direct assaults costly during the campaign.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670937/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Episode 529 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2026/05/31/civil-war-podcast-episode-529/"},{"label":"National Park Service battle summary","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=ga015"},{"label":"Library of Congress Kennesaw battlefield drawing","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660525/"},{"label":"Library of Congress Atlanta campaign map series","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2008628278/"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History campaign study","url":"https://history.army.mil/Publications/Publications-Catalog/The-Atlanta-and-Savannah-Campaigns/"}]}}]}]
